‎## V. YAHOO — HISTORICAL LEDGER
‎
‎**Case Study Control #1 — Dispatch 016**
‎
‎1990s: Yahoo was defining gateway — directory, portal, mail, search, advertising. Not struggling outsider.
‎
‎**Crossroads:**
‎
‎- Surface: Google or no Google? (acquire?)
‎- Deep: What business are we actually in? Destination or mechanism?
‎
‎Yahoo strength: portal — bring people to Yahoo and keep them inside.
‎Google strength: Find what user wants, then get out of way.
‎
‎Yahoo used Google search inside own results — evaluation produced behaviour — but narrative remained destination.
‎
‎Yahoo tried to build own search later — landscape already shifted.
‎
‎**Yahoo didn't lack:**
‎Resources, intelligence, access, opportunity. It had choices.
‎
‎**History became ledger of consequences.**
‎
‎Consequences visible years later. Crossroads didn't announce itself.
‎
‎> You don't always lose because you made wrong choice. Sometimes you lose because you correctly understood present — and incorrectly understood where road was going.
‎
‎**HakunaShortcut formulation:**
‎Destination optimizes for keeping user inside. Mechanism optimizes for initiating movement (command) then getting out of way. When forest grows, mechanism beats destination. Protocol beats interface.
‎
‎**Scored on Checklist 01-13:** Friction → complaints → evaluation (Yahoo used Google) → experimentation (Google as infrastructure) → diversification → portability failure → switching costs (identity as destination) → behaviour precedes declaration → competitor credible → network effects weaken → institutional response → narrative change → threshold.
‎
‎Lesson: Observe invariant. Preserve mechanism capability, not just destination.
‎
